        "person-peter-findley-fact-24"
      ],
      "text": "Peter Findley holds 42nd at 33.60, unchanged against the morning baseline. The commissioned feature above this recap asks a more durable question than whether he added points overnight. The desk’s October 11 check found Peter_Findley sharing the fewest recorded actions for a perfect win on all 25 public games. Those are shared minima across repeated play, not a claim of first-attempt perfection. Head human editor Mark Barney commissioned the analysis and supplies the account identification from his knowledge of Peter’s Discord handle; the feature’s editor note keeps that attribution distinct from independent profile verification."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Analysis: where does learned judgment enter the agent?",
      "sourceIds": [
        "dispatch-2026-10-11-peter-findley-human-mastery",
        "person-peter-findley-fact-11"
      ],
      "text": "Peter’s human record makes the engineering questions worth asking. Which observations help him reject a false rule? How does he decide that another experiment is worth the action budget, or that a reset is overdue? State tracking, memory and a reliable action interface could express that judgment in a harness; abstraction and experiment selection could express it in the model’s decisions. Making shared components work consistently may matter as much as inventing a component. These are possible explanations to investigate, not methods established for Peter’s submission. The valuable account would connect a concrete decision to a measured change in agent behavior."
    },
    {
      "heading": "A live research question, without a submission claim",
      "sourceIds": [
        "social-2109493530465059261",
        "social-2109420932955804040",
        "person-mike-knoop-identity-0"
      ],
      "text": "Mike Knoop raised a related question overnight, commenting that the same shift was happening with ARC-AGI-3 while quoting Pedro Domingos’s interpretation of harness-learning research. The linked paper describes learning to revise executable harnesses from feedback and reports reasoning and multi-hop question-answering experiments. Knoop supplied no named contestant or ARC experiment. His public comment adds context to the debate over where an agent’s competence resides; it does not explain Chen’s gain or Findley’s approach."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Beyond the top ten",
      "sourceIds": [
        "board",
        "wire-2026-10-11-1817-arc-3-hugo-thorvaldsen-surges-into-top-25",
